{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting","id":420749,"title":"Novas vs Psions, balance and setting","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-26T15:43:15-08:00","id":420749,"title":"Novas vs Psions, balance and setting","content":"Hi, sorry if this is an old idea, a bad one or just out of place.<br /> <br /> I stumbled upon an <a href=\"http://forum.rpg.net/showthread.php?664550-Trinity-reboot-What-do-you-want-to-see/page10\" target=\"_blank\">old discussion</a> on rpg.net which talked a bit about how psions could possibly stand up to aberrants/novas. There was some talk about psions somehow making each other stronger in larger groups, but most people wanted to keep the groups small (player group sized) and felt huge amounts of psions making mincemeat of aberrants wasn't a good idea.<br /> <br /> This clicked in my mind with the concept of psionic/subquantum powers interferring with nova/quantum ones. The result was the idea that each psi order could have the potential to interfere with a particular aspect of aberrant powers - say, a biokinetic/norca being able to prevent a nova from shapeshifting, an esper/ISRA to prevent a nova from using extrasensory perception, et cetera. That way, a small group consisting of members of most or all psi orders would have the potential of &quot;shutting down&quot; and defeating any one aberrant. One on one, unless the aberrant is very one-dimensional and encounters just the wrong psion, the aberrant is highly likely to win. Also, this creates an impetus for player groups/small diverse groups from various orders, possibly under the aegis of Aeon Trinity to help smooth over inter-order hostilities.<br /> <br /> Just my five cents, be seeing y'all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5180-from","name":"from","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-26T16:20:04-08:00","id":420796,"title":"","content":"Eh, balance kinda bugs me.<br /> <br /> In the original universe Novas and Aberrants are not the same thing. Aberrants, in Aeon, are twisted and broadly weaker decedents of what were once Novas. I could see some form of rules for a team of psions being able to resist Aberrant powers to a very limited degree, but I still want the Aberrant to have the edge against a team - it should be very hard to put down an Aberrant IMO.<br /> <br /> I think Novas shouldn't be anywhere near the 2120 setting, I prefer my Aeon as standalone pre the release of the other settings. If we <i><b>had</b></i> to have Novas in the Aeon setting, they should absolutely make complete sticky paste of most psions, team of them or otherwise. No contest.<br /> <br /> You can probably gather that I'm not a big fan of balance in all things (or Novas for that matter generally). Psions should have it tough, victory is all the sweeter if they win - and winning should not be a given even for a team versus a single Aberrant.<br /> <br /> I think of Aeon as marines versus Aliens (as in the movie Aliens). I don't see it as various flavors of supers / supervillains that should have any real balance.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":232,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","user":"CHILL","date":". 02-27-2015, 10:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-26T20:32:49-08:00","id":421144,"title":"","content":"I kind of have to agree with CHILL on this. Balance is boring. If it takes a group of players to take down some monster, then the players revel in it. the tougher the fight, then the better the stories of that session and specifically that battle. Now, I do think that the Nova should not be all uber-rargh and be able to mop the floor with the players instantly, but the players should shed blood when going up against a nova.<br /> Remember that, when it all comes down to it, this is a game and games are meant to be fun. Roleplaying games are also about bonding and a hard battle against a foe that should, speank you within seconds but you somehow beat, is an age-old trope of this genre.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-26T22:37:13-08:00","id":421330,"title":"","content":"I'm thinking I shouldn't have used the word balance, let me attempt to clarify what I meant by it.<br /> <br /> Chill, I'm with you 100% in your marines/Aliens comparison, only these Aliens are highly intelligent and were once human - they know us. We don't know them. I have no interest in making the players able to take down aberrants of increasing &quot;challenge rating&quot; or whatever.<br /> <br /> So, again - I'm not saying a player group should have a fair chance against an aberrant. I don't think they should have. There is a known in-universe effect of psion powers hindering aberrant powers, but it's ill defined. My suggestion is to define this to make members of each order potentially able to hinder one aspect of aberrant powers, perhaps with rules or with exact effects left to storyteller discretion, which would have the additional benefit of explaining player group-sized &quot;parties&quot; of members of different orders. If your squad isn't &quot;complete&quot;, you can't block/hinder some parts of the aberrant's powers. I.e., if you don't have an Upeo keeping it in place, it'll just blink out when you think you have it cornered.<br /> <br /> Also, since I'm thinking conspiracies, this would explain why mega-social aberrants first drove the Upeo away - they wanted to secure their own retreat.<br /> <br /> P.S. On top/Instead of that, perhaps members of orders should be immune/resistant to aspects of aberrant power in the same way? I.e., Legionnaires resist aberrant fire-based attacks better et cetera.<br /> <br /> P.P.S. Since I'm also an evil b*st*rd of a storyteller, I'm also thinking of requiring &quot;direct interaction with noetic template&quot;, i.e. you'll have to go hug the abbie to shut &quot;your&quot; part of its powers down. But I am also merciful, so if you did, I'd allow you to completely neutralise &quot;your&quot; part. Leading to the inevitable attempt at dogpiles, at which I'd laugh heartily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5180-from","name":"from","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-26T23:39:09-08:00","id":421375,"title":"","content":"Well, let's follow that through. What would Psychokinetics be able to interfere with? Other telekinetic attacks? The Upeo Ma Wacha could block teleportation. This supposed benefit is actually way too limited in scope. It is something that may, at some point, in some odd scenario be something that could be used.<br /> I have toyed around with something similar. For example, telepathy from a psion blowing right through the mind shields a nova might put up. Things that work on a noetic level tears right past quantum defenses like a comet through tissue paper. But I don't see a benefit to having them able to block a nova's powers that might fall into their purview. The kind of nova who has the ability to regenerate and shoot flame and shapeshift is really kind of small and narrow enough to not allow the dogpile scenario you are positing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-27T02:37:32-08:00","id":421521,"title":"","content":"I have always read 'the group effect' of Psions as meaning the doyen didn't want any individual (even the proxies) to singularly wield the true nature of their gift. The Prometheus Chamber is their 'soft-power' approach to the problem - disperse enough of their 'memes' into the awakened that the ideas join the human zeitgeist. So rather than single Psions having any effect on individual aberrants, what's happening is more like a greater diffusion of Psions (the sub-quantum particle) through the quantum medium and that's what makes high-taint aberrants in the 2120s less powerful than they expect (but still seriously powerful) to be. At least, in my own understanding. Tactical suppression as propaganda, mostly.<br /> -shrug-<br /> So system wise, i've rarely had reason to mess with how it was detailed. I never used Quantum/Psi suppression rules in any low number Psion encounter, when necessary to convey an atmosphere of Psionic suppression, I just improvised (ST fiat) weakened results (flight into clumsy high jumps, melee-range &amp; weakened Q-Bolt). I shoe-horned another escaped Huang-Marr Biorg (EK with teleporting auxiliary) into a parallel campaign from Darkness Revealed, with that foe i went with some very loaded house rules for the Biorgs surgically enhanced 'suppression field' - basically rendering the Aberrant PCs powerless inside of a stated range - as long as the enemy was aware of them.<br /> <br /> In my own personal experience, running many Nova characters through a 2120 setting, i've rarely really been bothered by any imbalance between the two as enemies. It's only the mixed inter-party balance that seemed problematic for me - using the Adventure! rules later on made it much more tenable - but lost the sheer volume of choice in character generation that the Aberrant style offered.<br /> Enforcing the low-Quantum novas from Asia Ascendant are the best way of crossing the styles over. These poor novas, erupted in the psionically cleansed 2120s (increasingly so after process 418), can be very human seeming (at first, at least) and, without combat specialization, are prime candidates for single PC beat downs (i think judge dredd vs mostly incompetent young spandex heroes/villains could be a fun role to play) by combat specialized Psions, as are a number of low-level sub-aberrants who mostly don't actually have access to true quantum power. Sub-aberrants could be made fairly pitiful and a decent combat party could see a number of kills each turn fresh out of character generation. But verifiable, experienced/notorious, quantum-tainted space-aberrants should scale between extremely dangerous (Anders Nash, Warfire) to plot device wielding deus ex machina (Apollo Milliken, all Prime Threats).<br /> <br /> But the idea of various Prometheus Chambers being used for specific suppressions is good, enough so that i'd like to use it at least - opposed to the general hand-wavery that noetic suppression of quantum taint as strictly background details. The one-for-one suppression rate leaves one side entirely powerless a good deal of the time and does get in the way of Aberrants being the scourge of known space - at least when using pure Aberrant-system nova generations (with increased taint levels in most cases). The few times an unsupported Psion or group encounter a decently powerful Aberrant - what could be a gripping escape/conflict becomes instead an anti-climax if a single full set of aptitude Psions with mid-level psi scores can significantly decrease the target's threat level.<br /> Each aptitude having its own specialty in the area is a delicious team-making element, like PK's cancelling out Mega-physical with ease, while EK's sort out the Q-Bolts and shields with cheap pulses. Some writings suggest the now defunct Chibs were the crown jewel of tactical suppression - with the unofficial supplement India Underground indicating they had routinely made Aberrant captures before their purge. But even there the system lacked the flair that a well thought out, aptitude approach could bring to the topic - maybe with simple sub-effects to each listed power when applied for taint suppression value. Just be cautioned to not go overboard, any climactic Aberrant encounter that doesn't lead players to seriously questioning their resolve isn't getting your money's worth out of the setting, as i understand it.<br /> The real-deal Aberrants of 2120 are boss-fights, often with spectacular set pieces and dramatic outcomes.<br /> I'd assume that beyond a certain level of permanent Quantum (or maybe Taint?), the Aberrant ought be immune to any small group suppression - simply to maintain that aspect of the setting balance. But considering some of the known intentions of the Doyen in distributing the Prometheus Chambers, some fun little detail like this for lower level taint encounters could be useful in penetrating both the Doyen war on taint and Aeon's message of unity into the system. Simply cancelling out the Aberrants powers makes it frustrating to play as the suppressed Aberrant, but messing the effects up and making them unpredictable is less so. Finally, nothing should really be able to cancel out an Aberrants permanent physical alterations, except perhaps forced Dormancy, which i think comes from India Underground(?).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-27T08:32:52-08:00","id":421785,"title":"","content":"Fair enough, perhaps I saw the balance part and focussed in on that <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I can see some potential for working through how psion abilities could affect other psions as a very limited dampening mechanism, that could be interesting and could make sense mechanically and from a contained story perspective (a Doyen fail-safe of sorts). I'd want to very heavily limit it though, no 'dispel-noetics' here, thanks. I'd probably not use it myself.<br /> <br /> OK, onto some form of story driven psion ability to mess with aberrant powers...... Nope. I wouldn't even make it a thing. There was some wishy-washy mention of psion powers dampening quantum powers that never got defined, and I was really glad it never did. I think it should stay that way. Psions are created to be weapons against aberrants, but just because I hold a gun doesn't mean the other guy's gun won't fire or be way, way bigger than mine. This resistance / neutralizing stuff feels like an attempt at watering down aberrants to me, or making psionic abilities stronger (something I <i><b>really</b></i> dislike).<br /> <br /> Its a personal preference sure, but I'm highly unlikely to use a 2120 era space-mad abbie in a Trinity game anyway apart from as a rare set piece encounter. If I do, I want those very rare times to hurt, a LOT. I want players mortally terrified of going anywhere near one of these things again. Psions should be the guys and gals that, although they are <i><b>very likely to die</b></i>, still go out and do this stuff<i><b> anyway</b></i>. That is far more interesting to me than making them more powerful / giving them some inherent edge against aberrants.<br /> <br /> Although the rules desperately needed work in 1st edition Aeon/Trinity, the rules were sufficient for psions and 2120 era aberrants to have at it. Complete with Psions coming out all kinds of broken at the other end of course, and probably dead. <i><b>Good</b></i>.<br /> <br /> More of this, please.<br /> <br /> Mostly though I just don't use Aberrants - I like to leave them as a background threat that psions should hope to never encounter and get on with using what I think is far more interesting material in the Aeon setting. There is so much rich material to use that doesn't include aberrants in 2120+ such as geopolitics, Chromatics, Qin, Doyen, mega-corps, government operatives, shades of gray in the Aeon Trinity, the UN.... not to mention the Orders and their many, many issues and hundreds of other things that have nothing to do with aberrants.<br /> <br /> Oh, and I never have to think about rules from the Aberrant books or Adventure and how they work in 2120 because I ignore all references to this stuff in later Trinity publications. Eden blew up, process 418 simply didn't happen. Its 2120 and psions are where its at <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":232,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","user":"CHILL","date":". 02-27-2015, 11:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-27T23:45:15-08:00","id":422660,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>thedonnie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=421375#post421375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> [...] This supposed benefit is actually way too limited in scope. It is something that may, at some point, in some odd scenario be something that could be used. [...]</div> </div> </div> </div>I have no issue with benefits/supposed benefits to Psions in Psion/aberrant conflicts is very limited in scope. I'm saying the game says their powers give them some benefit against aberrants and I came up with a way of handling that which makes some sense to me without tilting the scales in the Psions favor, which also happens to justify cross-order groupings adhering to the usual player-group model.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=421521#post421521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> [...] So rather than single Psions having any effect on individual aberrants, what's happening is more like a greater diffusion of Psions (the sub-quantum particle) through the quantum medium and that's what makes high-taint aberrants in the 2120s less powerful than they expect (but still seriously powerful) to be. [...]</div> </div> </div> </div>This is an interesting idea. With a bit of definition it could prove a useful guideline and if the effect is (still) localized (to, say, the earth), there'd be an excellent reason why taking the war to the aberrants is a very, very bad idea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=421521#post421521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> [...] I just improvised (ST fiat) weakened results (flight into clumsy high jumps, melee-range &amp; weakened Q-Bolt). [...]</div> </div> </div> </div>It sounds pretty much like you've been doing what I'm advocating, just by sheer storyteller experience, feel and fiat. Which is really the best way to do it, but a lot of us aren't good enough at either improvisation or player handling to get away with it - some rules/guidelines would be helpful.<br /> <br /> Also, I sort of like the by-aspect thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=421521#post421521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But the idea of various Prometheus Chambers being used for specific suppressions is good, enough so that i'd like to use it at least - opposed to the general hand-wavery that noetic suppression of quantum taint as strictly background details. [...] Just be cautioned to not go overboard, any climactic Aberrant encounter that doesn't lead players to seriously questioning their resolve isn't getting your money's worth out of the setting, as i understand it. [...]<br /> <br /> I'd assume that beyond a certain level of permanent Quantum (or maybe Taint?), the Aberrant ought be immune to any small group suppression - simply to maintain that aspect of the setting balance. [...] Simply cancelling out the Aberrants powers makes it frustrating to play as the suppressed Aberrant, but messing the effects up and making them unpredictable is less so. [...]</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm thinking something like your lowest node rating has to exceed the rating of the power you're trying to suppress. If I could use concepts from Aberrant, I'd say the Quantum requirement for the power you're trying to suppress. If your lowest node rating is high enough, you have to activate one of your own powers which can target others and successfully use it on the aberrant. If you do, rather than the normal effect, the abbies power doesn't work properly - ST fiat would probably be best here, since abbie powers can be so different, but I'd say a general guidelines would be remove a success from the abbie for every success you rolled.<br /> <br /> This is horrible, because you have to actively use and expend psi to suppress the aberrant every turn you suspect it might use the power you're trying to suppress. But it's something more than nothing.<br /> <br /> I've been thinking about the Sydney attack, and came to the conclusion that it consisted of a handful of abberants with &quot;terror capabilities&quot; (quantum bolt, immolation, elemental manipulation &amp; mastery) being dropped off as a &quot;hi, we're back&quot; by a more powerful aberrant. This turned out to match up very well with Legionnaire powers and quantum suppression/resistance, so the Legion only lost two or three psions for every aberrant they took down.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=421785#post421785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> [...]<br /> <br /> OK, onto some form of story driven psion ability to mess with aberrant powers...... Nope. I wouldn't even make it a thing. There was some wishy-washy mention of psion powers dampening quantum powers that never got defined, and I was really glad it never did.<br /> <br /> [...]<br /> <br /> Mostly though I just don't use Aberrants - I like to leave them as a background threat that psions should hope to never encounter and get on with using what I think is far more interesting material in the Aeon setting. [...]<br /> <br /> Oh, and I never have to think about rules from the Aberrant books or Adventure and how they work in 2120 because I ignore all references to this stuff in later Trinity publications. [...]</div> </div> </div> </div>i guess it depends on how you play the game. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I've always had the image of psions as humanity's defensive line against aberrants. Little actual conflict, but they're the main baddies. The aliens are mostly by-products, though of course urgent enough in their own right.<br /> <br /> Also, unlike you, I'm not quite able to just ignore the world-shattering powers in the Aberrant books. Seriously, how could a single order (Legion) hope to stop more than a single Nova/aberrant on the warpath, much less the full-scale attack on Sydney? I mean, Armor-5, Elemental Mastery-3+ and you have something that can shrug off most anything short of artillery while wreaking unearthly havoc all around it, and that's well within the limits of a starting character in Aberrant. And not a particularly optimised one.<br /> <br /> So I'd like that wishy-washy mention clarified. I like my own idea for it, but even something like &quot;psionic powers count as aggravated (?is this the right term? I don't have the books right now. Arnor-Piercing?) attacks against aberrants and quantum powers/defenses&quot; would make the universe more coherent in my mind.<br /> <br /> Thank you for the feedback! Particularly Nihilist, since you seemed interested in the idea, thus validating my genius. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> P.S. Sorry to all of you for cutting up your quotes so badly, but they are available higher up in the thread for anyone looking to see what you actually wrote. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5180-from","name":"from","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T05:11:41-08:00","id":422825,"title":"","content":"Novas should be horrendously powerful compared to Psions, IMO. That's a lot of the appeal for Aberrant as a game, and actually Psions really do need an enemy to face who outstrips them to the stage where a single Nova can fight half a dozen Psions and it could go either way (and that's not taking into account the notably powerful Novas!). Given the comic book inspiration behind Novas, they can't really be anything but absolutely earth-shaking in capability. Justice League level guys.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T05:46:12-08:00","id":422834,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422825#post422825\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Novas should be horrendously powerful compared to Psions, IMO. [...] Justice League level guys.</div> </div> </div> </div>I *REALLY* shouldn't have used the word &quot;balance&quot;. I agree with you 100%. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> However, I *do* recognise the rules stating that subquantum (psionics) somehow interferes with quantum (aberrant/nova), and I would like to have this more developed so I can know what to do with it. This interference, in my opinion, must be what allowed the Legion to defend Sydney, since a handful of Aberrant starter characters would just wade through an order of psions. Whether it adheres to my suggestion of &quot;like suppresses like&quot; or someting like Nihilist's idea that the higher saturation of psions make any aberrant (perhaps in particular around the earth?) weaker than they were during the Aberrant age, or some entirely different concept - I would like to know, as storyteller.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5180-from","name":"from","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T09:30:14-08:00","id":423037,"title":"","content":"Aplogies in advance for the length.<br /> -shame-<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=421785#post421785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although the rules desperately needed work in 1st edition Aeon/Trinity, the rules were sufficient for psions and 2120 era aberrants to have at it. Complete with Psions coming out all kinds of broken at the other end of course, and probably dead. <i><b>Good</b></i>.<br /> <br /> More of this, please.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you're talking about the loose guidelines from p303 of the Appendix in the Trinity core, i suppose they strictly are the best balance for the way the setting is presented. When the players are only privy to the Psion's rules, the enemies might as well be running on Latin alchemical processes - but there is nowhere near enough information to run Aberrant (or alien) PCs in those scant details in the Core-book. Plus, when you have vehicular Armor/Damage scaling the powers given in that section start to feel underwhelming. If i'm missing some source of rules for generating Aberrants in the 2120 setting (outside of options in Asia Ascendant), point them out. It's been a long time since i was entirely familiar with the full contents of my collection.<br /> -blush-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=421785#post421785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mostly though I just don't use Aberrants - I like to leave them as a background threat that psions should hope to never encounter and get on with using what I think is far more interesting material in the Aeon setting. There is so much rich material to use that doesn't include aberrants in 2120+ such as geopolitics, Chromatics, Qin, Doyen, mega-corps, government operatives, shades of gray in the Aeon Trinity, the UN.... not to mention the Orders and their many, many issues and hundreds of other things that have nothing to do with aberrants.<br /> <br /> Oh, and I never have to think about rules from the Aberrant books or Adventure and how they work in 2120 because I ignore all references to this stuff in later Trinity publications. Eden blew up, process 418 simply didn't happen. Its 2120 and psions are where its at <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, despite my forays into Aberrant 2120 campaigns, it breaks my heart to have so much of the rich setting cordoned off due to the parties preferred form of inspiration. So i feel i owe an explanation for how i've so deeply corrupted the pure Trinity 2120 setting. Because you better believe i revel in what's written whenever i can convince players to play the appropriate inspiration of the time.<br /> I've got to admit, from this and your posts in the Absolute Zero thread, you have me respecting your old-school approach to the setting. It was a few years before i got my hands on Aberrant and eventually Adventure!, so i never even considered them necessary - but i had (probably foolishly) planted the seeds of a renegade PC being an Aberrant instead of a latent PK training along legionary chamber prospects. The plan was to spin him off into a career as a space pirate for our customary parallel 'evil party' games, but i found the money to invest in Darkness Revealed &amp; Aberrant shortly after the PKs were dunked and the eruption event occurred.<br /> Afterwards, the two campaigns just went off in completely different (but cleverly reconcilable) directions only once ever interacting again, on the Tharsis frontiers of Mars - which is where those balance issues i mentioned were most stark. Out of curiosity, how do you use Aberrants in the manner you are recommending? All confined spaces and dramatic fudging? Could you give an example of how you personally go about putting together a challenging Aberrant encounter for a full party of middle-lower experience (10-20 xp) Psions? All just to appease my curiosity, honestly - i took an 'Ender's Game' approach to Aberrants before i had the 2008 setting in hand, none ever appeared for inspection and the propaganda was perceptibly suspect. I'm just wondering if you have actually run them off that one-page of practical rules in the appendix, or if you've taken an approach similar to my own back then...<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is an interesting idea. With a bit of definition it could prove a useful guideline and if the effect is (still) localized (to, say, the earth), there'd be an excellent reason why taking the war to the aberrants is a very, very bad idea.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I may venture into untested, possibly unplayable mechanics. But i'll wade in with some rules suggestions, with the caveat that i not be held responsible for any headaches incurred by my amateur ventures.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">a lot of us aren't good enough at either improvisation or player handling to get away with it - some rules/guidelines would be helpful.<br /> <br /> Also, I sort of like the by-aspect thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not sure what you mean when you indicate the &quot;by-aspect thing&quot;. Apologies.<br /> <br /> As for guidelines, i would suggest imagining you have that power in a dream - and at the critical moment in the dream the power is failing you. A lot of the time powers can still be used in some kind of reduced capacity, but sometimes more complex powers (level three powers and above, i used loosely) became too unstable to predictably wield. Much of this is accompanied by anecdotes of the session, drawn out over a few hours of thought, i've left it in to mess up the post and indulge in some nostalgia.<br /> Anecdotally; a PC with the level three Gravity Control/Mastery power suite experienced bizarre electro-magnetic slippage of the fundamental force involved, unlocking his vacuum-suit seals each time he tried - which while useless in conflict, was resourcefully used to unlock magnetic door seals during the escape (the aforementioned melee-range Q-Bolt was then used to punch out the locking mechanism wholesale, illustrating the PCs willingness to adapt to different powers without agonizing too much over what they've lost).<br /> Armor, Invulnerability and Mental/Sensory shields were mostly unaffected - but the flood of armed forces (comparatively few actual Psions) were equipped well enough to use various energy types to overcome those defenses case-by-case, so i didn't see the need. A particularly resilient Force-Fielder with some Armor and Invulnerability remained mostly unharmable, but was frantically failing to keep teammates safe and morally torn on whether to fight back.<br /> I started with the same idea about Force Fields but, eventually they began to blink out for enough time for an increasing number of blocked attacks to leak through. I was never called to task on why that happened, with everything else playing up. But it was truthfull re-balancing on the fly - they took no damage from a rocket-shell and enough was enough, the rocket was blocked, but shrapnel from debris penetrated the shield from a 90 degree gap on one side. It was much more satisfying reaction from the Force-Fielder as the second rocket was loaded, strictly from a ST perspective. -smile-<br /> Teleporting/Warping in was fine, but trying to leave the area is more difficult, particularly when employing Warp. I scared the PCs enough with the detectable Taint explosion every time one of those dirty quantum portals opened, that they didn't dare. But, i would imagine tele-severed limbs would have occured if any but the most Mega-Dextrous were to attempt passing through a roiling, unpredictable wormhole.<br /> The Mega-Physicals can mostly remain the same - maybe a cap on how many mega dice can be rolled for Dexterity, but dodging bullets is too fun to take away entirely when surrounded by armed assailants. Mega Strength is inherently problematic, I haven't been put in a position where i would need to think about it in regards to supression systems prior, but the nature of Mega-Strength's obsene number of automatic damage successes makes me think of overly baroque. nigh-unplayable rule solutions for 2120 Nova players. But from a Psion's perspective, a specialized, start-level Mega-Physical threat could bound in from the imperceptible distance, throw a punch that tears a vehicle in half, cartwheel-flip the ruin into distant support positions and jump back out of range, but after wards they are going to need days to recover that level of juice if those actions were performed under decent supression.<br /> Mega-Social is mostly useless when used on external targets in a supressed environment, the subtler effects are there (pained &amp; longing stares, easier verbal interactions, easily changed opinions) - but either supression, training, equipment or a combination of the three makes it as good as broadcasting visible taint clouds for all to see and isn't effective in anything but harmless diversion. Domination is the same, it works outside of the supression zone just fine, but is somehow more evident within the suppressed zones and easily discovered - maybe with specialized power enhancements, but mostly Quantum social controls aren't effective. I allowed the level one Hypnotism power to work with some greater subtlety, but still detectable under close inspection to any mid-strength Telepath. The other exceptions are when clever roleplaying comes up, when a player makes a good point or a persuasive arguement in character, for which you can temporarily suspend supression penalties in reward. A clever excuse by Aberrants slipping the net, whether plausible, intimidating or just entertaining to the group should always be a fun roll - no need to penalize that aspect of Mega-Social. As much as the team-against-overwhelming-odds RPG trope is applied above, i now apply the cleverer-than-thou trope to this exception.<br /> Because Mega-mental occurs so much inside the impenetrable Taint/Quantum-Matrix of the Aberrant mind, the Mega-Mental stats mostly remain unregulated by areas of suppression. This isn't always the same for taint-spewing quantum innovations, which can become buggy and failure prone inside the Psion-rich environment - indeed the safest places to store such dangerous black-tech is within a Psionically suppressed vault. Beloved Innovations can be shielded against this effect, but even then minor systems failures help synthesize the urgency and desperation of Aberrant actions under heavy suppression.<br /> <br /> Just off the top of my head, that's most of the calls i think i had to make - it was a single night's game close to a decade ago now... -smile- The precise location this level of suppression was encountered was the Qin Embassy in Olympus responding to suspected attack by Aberrants, in case you wonder how severe a Psionic infusion i was indicating. None of the PCs had greater than four Quantum at the time, i expect a few more points might have rendered the field more irritant than obstacle. I later indicated that a villain (disembodied Anders Nash) had enough Quantum to use cyberkinesis on the embassy during the event because of the measurable disruption the Aberrants had caused in the sub-quantum field - but this was Mega-Intelligence jargon to a certain extent.<br /> Beyond this i could go into details, but i would be focused on the Aberrant perspective of the puzzle, which may not be your desired topic. To add awkwardness, i only ever really spent much time with the free-form psionics from the Trinity Players Guide, so i'm rather drawing a blank what the official mode-level powers even were...<br /> -blush-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm thinking something like your lowest node rating has to exceed the rating of the power you're trying to suppress. If I could use concepts from Aberrant, I'd say the Quantum requirement for the power you're trying to suppress. If your lowest node rating is high enough, you have to activate one of your own powers which can target others and successfully use it on the aberrant. If you do, rather than the normal effect, the abbies power doesn't work properly - ST fiat would probably be best here, since abbie powers can be so different, but I'd say a general guidelines would be remove a success from the abbie for every success you rolled.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wait, Aberrant M-R Nodes? Used to supress Quantum? Are we using the same nomenclature here? I wish i knew what you were getting at because the Quantum Requirement of a power is actually a effective variable to be referencing in a proposed solution. Node is an Aberrant Background which can be used as a rudimentary version of Attunement as possessed by Aptitude Psions, as well as providing the Aberrant with greater Quantum replenishment rates - but longer psionic detection range. Psions don't have the...<br /> Nodes... Wait, do you mean Aptitude <u>M</u>odes?<br /> <br /> You are talking about individual suppression here, ok. i got trolled. -smile-<br /> We may have some fundamental disagreements with how Psionic Suppression works, because I don't see any Non-Proxy, Non-Huang-Marr Biorg having enough individual juice to move the needle on environmental sub-quantum physics and apart from Quantakinesis, sub-quantum Psionic powers and Quantum Taint are like oil and water - but i'll play along with the example. Tell me if i'm misunderstanding you here:<br /> - So if your lowest Mode is (say) Vitakinesis Mentatis (or Empathy if you want it Aptitude restricted) at 1, you can apply it to a suspected target of Quantum Hypnosis (Quantum Requirement 1).<br /> - So now we activate a targeted power, which is fine with free-form Psi, but may have trouble translating into the pre-written modes.<br /> - Now take the turn activating the effect - most rolls for powers involve Psi. A range of 1-10, 4 successes being the best average possible.<br /> - The Psi-Mode effect is instead used to resist the anticipated Quantum-Power success for success - potentially reducing the Q-Power effect to zero successes.<br /> <br /> If i didn't understand this 'Node/Mode' business, i'd appreciate a rephrasing to try again - because the Quantum Requirement is enticingly elegant as a target of supression. -shrug- Although i would have used it differently, if i understand what you were getting at.<br /> I warned above one-for-one cancellation above, this is more or less 'Dispel Noetics' as CHILL warned against, give or take.<br /> As for a hard-numbers alternative, maybe start with the Aberrant's Quantum score, then subtract the Psionic field rating, probably best indicated by an individual's Psi score, if you want to do individual supression - the result is X for simplicities sake.<br /> Then see if the Quantum power being used requires a Quantum level higher than X (and if you're concerned with Aptitude check to see if the Power falls under the Aptitude's Suppression criteria, if so the power is affected by chaotic misbehaviour.<br /> But this leads to individual Aptitude Psions being able to completely weirdify all potential Quantum-Powers. So I don't like it.<br /> Maybe if the Psionic Field rating was limited to five - maybe use (Psi / 2) or the lowest Mode, like you suggested. So the lowest X can get is five.<br /> Maybe further psions acting to supress could add one to the field generated by their strongest member if they joined the effort, so a group of six could effectively weirdify all power levels and potentially represent a number of different Aptitudes for a diverse set of supressions.<br /> This is overpowered as hell, but it's not final in the slightest - just public. -rolls eyes-<br /> So, rather than resisting rolls, participating psions must make at least one Meditation success each round and spend one temporary Psi, making splitting the action pool a tempting risk. With more Aptitude Psions to back you up, you could let others take your place once tired - leading to an interesting visual and if kept up long enough - could be used to force any level of Aberrant to flee. But during this time, the target Aberrant is still able to dish out massive auto-successes once they figure out ways to adapt their lower level and more reliable powers - distracting them with well-armored and armed forces always needs to be part of a rounded Aberrant assault strategy.<br /> The range of this effect is important too, like you inferred with direct template interface - group-hugs aren't always a wise move in tactical combat. Attunement ranges are a bit extreme, although some biotech device linking a Clear in with a supression squad to suppress at those ranges and even greater might be feasible. The naked Suppression Squad should be restricted by visual range at the furthest and most reliable within a few hundred meters. But, my motivation to arrive at this exact number eludes me at this time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is horrible, because you have to actively use and expend psi to suppress the aberrant every turn you suspect it might use the power you're trying to suppress. But it's something more than nothing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, we all come up with stinkers now and then. Even when it looks good on paper, it takes a number of convoluted uses in game to really judge something worthy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been thinking about the Sydney attack, and came to the conclusion that it consisted of a handful of abberants with &quot;terror capabilities&quot; (quantum bolt, immolation, elemental manipulation &amp; mastery) being dropped off as a &quot;hi, we're back&quot; by a more powerful aberrant. This turned out to match up very well with Legionnaire powers and quantum suppression/resistance, so the Legion only lost two or three psions for every aberrant they took down.</div> </div> </div> </div>This was basically my take too, something like; eight of the Colony's pure-bloods warp into the airport, three are precious warpers who either warp out immediately, or escape any supression field generated by the Legion presence before warping. Maybe one remaining Aberrant attacker has Warp capabilities for a wishful-thinking withdrawal, about five real Novas take the field of battle - heavy hitters and showboats most likely, some of the Colony's main 'generals'. The Warps also bring in a throng of sub-aberrants - which is what you see mostly on the mural pictured in the books. But the reality of the battle was more like; all the Legions vs. five Aberrants who were probably aware of the accumulated local Suppression field as soon as they got there and likely escaped after an opening volley. If any were actually cornered and taken down by the Legions, they must have been of unknown or politically sensitive origins to have not been named or pictured in the mural and accompanying documents.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">i guess it depends on how you play the game. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I've always had the image of psions as humanity's defensive line against aberrants. Little actual conflict, but they're the main baddies. The aliens are mostly by-products, though of course urgent enough in their own right.</div> </div> </div> </div>You may find Khantze Lu Ge an interesting location, I don't know if Yog-Death or Kali (i hope i'm remembering those names right) are pumping out the same brand of sub-aberrant as the Colony, but the rate of human and Aberrant interaction on KLG are the most intense in 2120 space.<br /> Personally, i view the premier enemies of the Trinity as the Doyen, who have done all they can to entrench and widen the divide between humans and the Taint afflicted. The aberrant war was a tragic civil war, but the Doyen see it only in terms of abnormality or undesired fold in their dimensional perception. There are less wasteful solutions to cure the tainted dealt with in Aberrant books (Teragen, perhaps?), the era of the war was all kinds of messed up, even Aeon was stagnant - the war generation were fighting over a whole can of worms that is misrepresented in the service of unifying the survivors under the laster symbol of fear from that era - the cosmic perversions that walked among us and were us.<br /> But the truth was (hopefully -hint-) much more interesting, for every Colony or Geryon that left, a number of Anteaus' and Firemen were also cast out from the only home they'd ever known. It's sympathetic and the events that caused the mass eruption events were caused by Novas in the know - blindly suffusing physical space with Taint. The stalwarts of Adventure! might have left some taint about, but with the greater number of Mesmers about, any problem was self-correcting. What little is known about Process 418 suggests it's also a balanced event, unlike the Taint/Quantum-heavy bursts of the Aberrant era.<br /> It would be interesting to see how the Doyen reacted to that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, unlike you, I'm not quite able to just ignore the world-shattering powers in the Aberrant books. Seriously, how could a single order (Legion) hope to stop more than a single Nova/aberrant on the warpath, much less the full-scale attack on Sydney? I mean, Armor-5, Elemental Mastery-3+ and you have something that can shrug off most anything short of artillery while wreaking unearthly havoc all around it, and that's well within the limits of a starting character in Aberrant. And not a particularly optimised one.<br /> <br /> So I'd like that wishy-washy mention clarified. I like my own idea for it, but even something like &quot;psionic powers count as aggravated (?is this the right term? I don't have the books right now. Arnor-Piercing?) attacks against aberrants and quantum powers/defenses&quot; would make the universe more coherent in my mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>That difference struck me as interesting too when i first got the Aberrant book - at first it seemed a bit camp, but perhaps because of that i looked in and found a jumble of powers and abilities that struck me as inversely reminiscent of Call of Cthulhu - because you were playing <i>as</i> the unstoppable monster in this game. It was the 'Monster Manual' for the Trinity setting, even more so with the Player's Guide powers - some of which are there in line with Trinity era historical events.<br /> <br /> I used Aggravated damage in Trinity despite the rules never mentioning it (i beleive), only ever caused by Taint. So it came up once, during the encounter with Anders Nash who dealt Aggravated (direct template) damage with Disintegration - which lead to a cool story and a replacement biotech arm for the victim. There are no specific provisions for the healing of such damage with Vitakinetics, so i never really went down that path again with the Psion campaigns. But it added to the mystique and gravity of Nash, so if i were to ever run the Freya encounter again, 'Aggravated' damage would be back on the table.<br /> As for Psions dishing damage out past Aberrant soaks? I'm not sure that's right for the type of encounter these things should represent, but i understand the urge to lower those astounding levels of soak - it can sometimes be helpful to apply the damage add after the soak has been subtracted for the human/Psion side of those conflicts, because they escalate quickly into vehicular battles. None-the-less, this can be satisfying for the world-of-cardboard Aberrant player and i'm sure could be made into a hectic save-the-world Psion player encounter too.<br /> Despite the combat Nova being the top choice amongst my own players, i'm aware that isn't representative of the greater nova population - most of whom are stuck with crummy low-quantum powers that may not even do anything helpful for them and will invariably lead to being mercilessly hunted if discovered. So when flying, force-fielded Q-Bolt spammers or armored, mega strong laser-eyed kaiju show up, they are exceptions and not the rule. If anything there is a kind of natural selection bias toward stealth and social climbing to avoid detection - these ghosts and sphinxes are the greater challenges to dislodge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422660#post422660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thank you for the feedback! Particularly Nihilist, since you seemed interested in the idea, thus validating my genius. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Since you've singled me out here, i feel inclined to bury you under the exhausted rants of someone who thinks entirely too much about this stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T12:42:55-08:00","id":423325,"title":"","content":"Wow this thread started shifting. OK, trying to catch up - apologies for missing anything. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>From</b><br /> Also, unlike you, I'm not quite able to just ignore the world-shattering powers in the Aberrant books. Seriously, how could a single order (Legion) hope to stop more than a single Nova/aberrant on the warpath, much less the full-scale attack on Sydney? </div> </div> </div>Its right there in the Aeon core book, and I'm able to ignore the Aberrant books <i><b>very</b></i> easily. First, Nova's and Aberrants are not the same thing. The world shattering powers in the Aberrant books aren't a thing for me because the Aberrant book wasn't published when I started running Trinity, and I didn't see anything that interested me enough to butcher my 2120 games dragging its god-awful over-the-top super powers into my 2120 games. In the Aeon core there were some rules for 2120 era Aberrant antagonists, it made them sufficiently powerful enough to be a challenge for a low-mid powered group of psions and that was and remains enough for me. Those Aberrant template rules in the Aeon core book are how I approach Aberrants in 2120. Sure, I read the Aberrant line and played Aberrant myself several times, but haven't once used anything from it in any of it in my 2120 games when GMing. When I felt the Trinity line started losing the plot (Eden, Process 418 as published), I simply ignored that stuff and moved on. Novas are all dead or disappeared off into their own pocket universes in a puff of smoke or whatever the heck was needed to keep them out of my games, effectively leaving their Aberrant descendants behind. Novas were a relic from the 21st century and a non-issue by 2120.<br /> <br /> I've never once had a problem with this in a game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Solar</b><br /> Given the comic book inspiration behind Novas, they can't really be anything but absolutely earth-shaking in capability. Justice League level guys. </div> </div> </div>And that right there is why they buggered off in a puff of smoke long before 2120 in my Aeon games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>From</b><br /> This interference, in my opinion, must be what allowed the Legion to defend Sydney, since a handful of Aberrant starter characters would just wade through an order of psions. </div> </div> </div>Hrm, actually gotta disagree on that one, and I guess this is where I get to the central topic of the thread . A sufficient number of well armed legion psions could (with heavy losses) survive an attack from Aberrants (as detailed in the the Aeon core book page 303). If you start wanting to use Nova powers from the Aberrant book instead of that then well yeah, of course. But like I said, Novas and Aberrants are very different from each other, and I think trying to use Nova powers is what makes this a problem in the first place. There were no Nova powers in play at Syndey.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Nihilist</b><br /> If you're talking about the loose guidelines from p303 of the Appendix in the Trinity core, i suppose they strictly are the best balance for the way the setting is presented. When the players are only privy to the Psion's rules, the enemies might as well be running on Latin alchemical processes - but there is nowhere near enough information to run Aberrant (or alien) PCs in those scant details in the Core-book. </div> </div> </div>And? In my Aeon games you play Psions or neutrals. Period. No proto-psions, no aberrants, no aliens and definitely, absolutely no bloody novas (remember, puff of smoke) <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Nihilist</b><br /> I've got to admit, from this and your posts in the Absolute Zero thread, you have me respecting your old-school approach to the setting. </div> </div> </div>*puts on some 80s rock* <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Seriously - thanks. Again coming to Aeon first, and having little interest in a god-games (Aberrant) or pulp games (A!), its really easy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Nihilist</b><br /> Out of curiosity, how do you use Aberrants in the manner you are recommending? All confined spaces and dramatic fudging? Could you give an example of how you personally go about putting together a challenging Aberrant encounter for a full party of middle-lower experience (10-20 xp) Psions? All just to appease my curiosity, honestly </div> </div> </div>Sure - well, kinda. This may drive people a bit crazy, but this works for me.<br /> <br /> Its probably worth communicating the caveat that I really don't get excited about or interested in specific rules or stat blocks or the mechanics of how this armor is more effective against that gun or power when I'm GMing. Your average player will focus on that stuff enough. Of course I'll adjudicate or explain how various rules work to players make sure they have some structure and know when to expect from a mechanics perspective as things relate to their characters. Its my job as GM to be there for them on that stuff, but for the bits <i><b>I</b></i> need to do - that stuff takes far less priority.<br /> <br /> Onto specifics. Let's see if I can communicate this clearly enough (apologies if I fluff it, which I probably will).<br /> <br /> I don't think I've ever sat down and worked out stat blocks for NPC's in advance if i can find a base in the core book and simply change my flow in-game as I need. Maybe I did back in my 2nd Ed DnD days for a brief time, but now its way easier to just alter the flow as I go, using published stats as a baseline.<br /> <br /> I've only used aberrant antagonists when presented in the published scenarios, pretty much using the rules for them as presented in those books as a base. I make a lot up on the fly in those kinds of encounters, reacting to players / PCs and how the session itself is playing out. As those encounters tend to be set-pieces, I usually fudge the dice roll results and go for what is dramatic, as I mentioned in another thread.<br /> <br /> Any home-made games have been entirely absent any aberrants though, so I can't honestly answer that part of your question. While I don't fudge dice roll results for non set-piece encounters, I do modify stat blocks on the fly for routine / filler encounters to alter the flow of play a bit.<br /> <br /> Perhaps this helps explain how I tackle all combat encounters outside set-pieces, and would likely tackle throwing abbies at a low-end psion group. No matter the relative power level of a PC group, I usually have some basic stat block somewhere in one of the books as a rough base then alter the flow as I go.<br /> <br /> So, lets take the Proteus Agent template in the core book. If the party is jumped by a bunch of these guys, I'll use the basic stats to get the combat going, but over successive rounds may dial down their effectiveness by (for example) reducing the soak dice if the party is having a rough time. I may also dial up the effectiveness of a couple of the agents on the fly (say increase their Firearms pool by a couple dice) if I want this to be a little more difficult.<br /> <br /> I've learned that this still feels natural to the players. I've actually tested this theory multiple times. I've run encounters strictly by the book with no changes to stats, and also using my 'flow' approach. No-one had the faintest idea which was which, although the stat-adhering approach felt more constraining for me whenever I did this.<br /> <br /> The flow approach makes for a much faster game and ton less prep-work for me. My game prep is maybe 80% story, 17% coffee, and 3% checking rules (out of which 0.01% is reviewing stat blocks, if I can be arsed).<br /> <br /> That make any sense?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":232,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","user":"CHILL","date":". 02-28-2015, 03:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T13:17:57-08:00","id":423363,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>from</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=422834#post422834\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I *REALLY* shouldn't have used the word &quot;balance&quot;. I agree with you 100%. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> However, I *do* recognise the rules stating that subquantum (psionics) somehow interferes with quantum (aberrant/nova), and I would like to have this more developed so I can know what to do with it. This interference, in my opinion, must be what allowed the Legion to defend Sydney, since a handful of Aberrant starter characters would just wade through an order of psions. Whether it adheres to my suggestion of &quot;like suppresses like&quot; or someting like Nihilist's idea that the higher saturation of psions make any aberrant (perhaps in particular around the earth?) weaker than they were during the Aberrant age, or some entirely different concept - I would like to know, as storyteller.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's always been my belief that power nullification is a cheap cop-out move. I'd make it so that Psions in groups are able to combine their abilities to the extent that they can contain, fight and defeat Novas. Novas are no less powerful when Psions are around and Psions do not have powers which are specifically able to overcome Nova's defences, but Psions in large groups are able to direct forces which can contest Novas and when this is combined with judicious use of advanced technology and tactics, it means that Novas can be defeated by sufficient numbers of Psions working in concert.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T14:24:30-08:00","id":424516,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=423325#post423325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But like I said, Novas and Aberrants are very different from each other, and I think trying to use Nova powers is what makes this a problem in the first place. <b>There were no Nova powers in play at Syndey.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>You really hit the nail on the head here, the difference between 2120 Aberrants and 2008 Novas are just painfully obvious. There were no Nova powers in play at Sydney, the images of the Colony returning to attack Vesta are tantalizing, but beyond surviving in vacuum and fighting off the stations (presumedly) weak defenses in a full-scale assault, we aren't really given any reason to assume these are comic-level super-gods.<br /> The images of the attacks of Mars or Luna <i>can</i> more potentially feed into either mythos - either the giant slug thing is a specialized 2120 aberrant version of a tank or some frightful transformation of a previously human creature swollen and malformed in a warning against too much power. But let's assume the former, they are under heavy artillery fire and vehicular scaled resistance by this stage. Now there is no stated reason the core appendix version of aberrants can't have twelve stamina and a three high stack of the armor option each, but that's much more arbitrary and hand wavey than stacking Force Field over Armor (or appropriate Invulnerability) and mega-Stamina - which may have seperable elements to deal with. But how are the psions going to do any better against these 25+ soak monsters? Why are they going to do any better than the IPF-Delta frigates, that have a fifteen point armor add when the acid option listed will do up to eight dice of lethal. The Osner ATV has ten points of armor add, neither can be affected by the strongest listed power in the appendix. So they were even internally inconsistent back then as to how the encounter was intended - at least using the standard of Anders Nash to judge.<br /> This creates a two-fold problem for those of us wishing to reconcile the settings (because they both <i>can</i> play a role with some out-of-the-box imagination). Firstly, was this is all happening in the same contuinity? That's what got us to buy the book off of a campaign of minimal promotion (at least from my perspective), isn't it? Huge disconnect here from a supposed prequel, so we try to cross them over to get our money's worth - all with dirty little house rules and dubious interpretations of scant details sifted from the greater three-piece work. But it forces us away from the 'Pure' setting you so lovingly tend, not because we aren't drawn to it as it was written - quite the oposite, we want to get every thing we can out of it - all of it.<br /> Secondly, these are really two different game-systems lurking beneath the dot-happy white-wolf exterior. Being interested in the setting almost invariably leads to in-house rules fabrication, as story concepts from one era seep into another. I was slow enough to take this one square to the face in my own personal games, being too proud to admit i had just lead the game off a developers chasm. So those of us experiencing this gap due to our own insistence to unite the (well beloved, i gather) metaplot have been forced out of the pure Trinity comfort zone.<br /> This is heartbreaking at times, the concept of a lost half-century, the idea of Divis Mal waiting around every corner plotting revenge, the idea of the human race as a single protagonist against the tainted stars and any aliens that get on our bad side. They are all wonderful elements that are either taken off the table, re-interpreted or cast into shadow by the greater scope of things. The 'cast into shadow' part appealed to me (WOD lead me to TU, apologies to the world of brightness players), so i was able to get through it - but for at least a few months i was terribly torn over whether to cauterize the greater continuity away from my precious SF game. It was only after seeing all the possibilities for Aberrant PCs in Descent into Darkness that i really went in for the greater scope.<br /> Sorry for so many words, but i think i just figured out why i have so much to say about it - it was those months where i was deciding which way to go. Nobody else i knew owned the setting, i wasn't really using the internet right back then and i had no one to talk to about it. When a few others finally got their own look into the setting, i was there ready with a good year of thought and research to answer questions for them, but few ever questioned my base assumptions - which may be why i now leap to defend/examine them. Maybe i was wrong all along.<br /> -shrug-<br /> Whatever it is, i hope some of my self-exploration is helping rather than create a form of text-pollution.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=423325#post423325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And? In my Aeon games you play Psions or neutrals. Period. No proto-psions, no aberrants, no aliens and definitely, absolutely no bloody novas (remember, puff of smoke) <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>It's like the first breath of fresh air i've had in years... I'm glad i opened the window. I'm reminded of early planning for a party of space pirates and the ranking up of Legionaires driven to go out on the first Leviathan trips even as grunts.<br /> -smile-<br /> I'm glad you are here to keep the Trinity setting by itself in perspective CHILL, it must get lonely sometimes with all the adaption and weirding some of us have taken to be head-canon over the years. How far are you willing to compromise on some of this stuff? Would you have any issue with the meta-plot if the rules didn't create so many questions? Where do you draw the cut-off line for the Trinity supplements compromising too much to the greater trilogy of settings, you've already mentioned Eden? But, perhaps these are questions best left for another thread...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=423325#post423325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*puts on some 80s rock* <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Seriously - thanks. Again coming to Aeon first, and having little interest in a god-games (Aberrant) or pulp games (A!), its really easy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Apart for Call of Cthulhu, my 'god' gaming is limited to aberrants in 2120 with all of collected humanity against them. I even ran WOD mostly as low-powered street-games - running those 'Tainted Novas' in 2120 is really unprecedented for me and it would count for something that a purist like you know that - because i was almost down that path myself if it weren't for being too fascinated with the bias against aberrants in the core book back when it was the ONLY source. Simply because i personally skew 'robin hood' over 'knights of camelot'.<br /> I did eventually find a pretty sweet balance with PCs as low-quantum stealth Novas out of Asia Ascendant, but that's probably out of your wheel-house too - pity, because i would love to have heard how the Nihonjin Remnants could have mixed with your Fuyoushi setting, due to my own campaign experiences. Like i mentioned in another thread, the opportunity was there at one point and those abandoned tunnels were very tempting.<br /> -shrug-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=423325#post423325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The flow approach makes for a much faster game and ton less prep-work for me. My game prep is maybe 80% story, 17% coffee, and 3% checking rules (out of which 0.01% is reviewing stat blocks, if I can be arsed).<br /> <br /> That make any sense?</div> </div> </div> </div>I cut a lot out here, because it absolutely makes sense. I learned to ST/DM/GM/whatever from basic D&amp;D when i was twleve, anything i can't hold in my memory without careful research is a bit more work than i like to be forced to do when improvising. I tend to spend a bit more time on rules (and much less on coffee -smile- Now Natsuko makes more sense. -tease-) than you, because after putting together some visual aids and NPCs with a basic flowchart of obvious choices worth of story i tend to improvise most of what we play anyway - very often in complete disregard for what i've actually prepared and Trinity stat-blocks/technology can be made pretty universal with a little improv. Your approach to every possible aberrant combination would mean ALL NOVAS being reduced to single page of rules - which i would be able to improv the crap out of.<br /> -pleasant daydream-<br /> Once the players had seen behind the curtain for 2008 style 'Aberrants' it just became too much wrangling to put the genie back in the bottle - i have still managed to get two run-throughs of Darkness Revealed started outside of that madness (one of which had to accept an Adventure! style 'daredevil' however -sigh-) - but apart from the set-piece of Anders Nash on the Freya (which leaves little room for ST improv) and the complex RP issues of the Huang-Marr project - these issues almost never come out in Trinity Pre-Eden. So i just had to ask, i suspect playing Trinity the way it was envisioned makes 'Battleground' and it's unpublished Player's Guide, rather than 'Aberrant' the more authoritative source for 2120 Aberrants - which i (disrespectfully) call sub-aberrants in my own interpretation.<br /> That nomenclature will have to change then, i guess...<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=423363#post423363\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's always been my belief that power nullification is a cheap cop-out move. I'd make it so that Psions in groups are able to combine their abilities to the extent that they can contain, fight and defeat Novas. Novas are no less powerful when Psions are around and Psions do not have powers which are specifically able to overcome Nova's defences, but Psions in large groups are able to direct forces which can contest Novas and when this is combined with judicious use of advanced technology and tactics, it means that Novas can be defeated by sufficient numbers of Psions working in concert.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much my take in general - Tactical Psionic Supression of Taint is propaganda, a fairy tale for the un-dipped.<br /> It shows up in popular media and recruitment pushes, but is never talked about seriously on a battlefield - maybe the Chibs made that different, but with India Underground being unofficial it can be hard to tell what the original intentions were. I made some concessions toward localized sub-quantum fields - but i felt that these were more a product of Doyen planning/manipulation than of conscious human agency. My suggestions to 'from' above are how i might imagine it works on Strike Force Psion - in some strikingly co-ordinated, visually 'enhanced' display for audiences of potential recruits.<br /> India Underground makes mention of Chitra-Bhanu lead aberrant captures that require intense inter-order co-operation - but never expanded on the idea in clear rules (as far as i remember). So, i guess you could synthesize a system based on something like the 'Noetic Crystallizers' from that source (India Underground page 61 by my reader's count), because siphoning off quantum pool is a great way to finish - but the problem is how to start without a Chib as i understand.<br /> I would probably make concessions to Quantum-Powers in non-freeform psi in any new edition of rules if it were up to me - because, damn, that one-on-one is just embarrassing - if it was between two PCs, one is coming away with lowered confidence (and possibly a bad experience in what should be fun) and we can predictably guess which one it will be. Not too much, mind, aberrants are the terrors of known space and besides some frustration a well rounded Quantum wielder should be able to adapt, but something to buy a turn or make it easier to target something else would be better than the nothing they currently have for most Psions. Maybe a seperate dueling system would work, like psionic combat in D&amp;D psionics, but it would have to include a lot of other stuff, so i won't be personally advocating that idea any further lest that work fall on me.<br /> -distancing self from dueling system-<br /> When you say &quot;advanced technology and tactics&quot;, i'm reading &quot;orbital weapons platforms and 1000-to-1 ratio of manpower and resources or greater&quot;. Even century old, ten quantum war-dogs are going to run low on quantum eventually - you corner them at the right moment and it's just a question of how much firepower to bring to bear. Well, with some adjustment to soaking mechanics at least...<br /> <br /> Even in my own experience no concessions further than <b>allowing a turn of damage to be calculated as a single soakable total for included attacks</b> (hand-waved using 2120 computer gunnery, potentially with some clairsentient-telepathic-networking if needed) are necessary for the heavier attack vehicles to out-damage even the most loaded 2008-style Nova build. But that concession <b>has</b> needed to come up a few times at the highest levels i ever let it get to (a few NPC Q6-8 veterans of the aberrant war specialized in combat who could sometimes soak any single weapon in the 2120 setting with no damage, literally game-breaking, but vital elements of a few set pieces). I also found myself switching the order of damage and damage add in the soaking process when the Aberrants were defending against vehicular scaled attacks, but even with all that in their favour individual vessels were still glass cannons serving as pawns and requiring a well-funded, -concerted and -planned effort against real threats. Mostly those NPCs were capable of taking on whole armies and living to fight another day in almost all cases - when the PCs started to beleivably challenge <i>them</i>, i knew it was time to pull the plug. The system wasn't written for cross-overs. But the stories were all there, lined up and ours for the taking - which kept things rolling long enough to get to that stage and i could have kept going if the framework was there. The scales were wasteful and the rules were broken, but the setting thrived under the inverted perspective and little effort was needed on my side to find space or motivations for 2120 aberrants - enough that i tried again later with more realistic expectations and better understanding of the complete setting. I'd even give it a third try, if the players wished.<br /> <br /> But in one on one PHYSICAL combat with Psions? No. Even the high hopes i placed on a custom-Varg fighter-ace with the house-rules above were dashed out in a few scant rounds. A single psion against a party of (relatively) low-powered novas became a pet project, for a while then.<br /> When eventually i went nuclear on the house-rules for Huang-Marr biorgs (free-form psi based Mega-Psi, no aptitude restrictions, fully replenished doubled psi pool each turn, psionic version of Mega-Wit's quickness, and so on...) the 2120 'Novas' <i>finally</i> had to think out of the box and work together though, enough that i was already planning for another couple of escaped biorgs breaking out of canon to show up later on - i flirted with one being skilled in QK - which might have been the measure to really put it over the edge into nightmare material for Novas. But that was fighting fire with fire and even i admit it was extreme and unhealthy for the system as written - but made somehow necessary by the ease the PCs dispatched the canon threats to aberrant invaders with. If i had lined up all the Novastorm IACruisers in a single place at once, a fresh &lt;100xp team might have been able to take them all out in a single well-planned attack of their own - which just begged for <b>some</b> re-balancing. But mostly, i just made sure no-one was ever dumb enough to make such a rendezvous publicly available intel - same for the Leviathans, but even more so.<br /> It should probably be noted that this was in a 2120s Tainted Nova campaign, where these conflicts took place much more regularly than in any well-paced Trinity game. Further, the Nova PCs who were capable of this level of Quantum are explicitly rare - the elder aberrants who smuggled them off earth and outwards gathered less than seven in an unstated number of years. Mostly the newer 'Novas' of 2120 are low-quantum, stooges or thugs for the 4th legion and other extremists to pick on for media events, in my head-canon.<br /> <br /> Look at me, still going, and we haven't even touched on the more subtle Quantum effects either. That's a much more nasty can of worms, leaving you wondering how many humans still actually possess free-will in 2120 between the Doyen, Telepaths and Q-powered remnants. I touched on it when i mentioned how i ran the suppression field around the Qin embassy - but that was a pretty specialized scene where the characters knew they were probably getting in too deep. I was quietly doing everything i could to sabotage their (understandably) growing confidence over psion/human resistance, playing to their expectations was predictable but healthy for the tipping balance. Which is why i hope to find some useful, but relatively unobstusive game-mechanic in this thread.<br /> -shrug-<br /> If there was a framework for the Onyx Path edition we could be using that instead - is there somewhere i can get a light version of the system for discussion or be there a pay wall to negotiate?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T20:08:56-08:00","id":424798,"title":"","content":"Can we all just take a moment to pause and admire the sheer dedication Nihilist has to this thread. Damn, I need more coffee... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Its late, so I'll keep this brief (compared to Nihilist, lol!) and want to avoid getting too far off subject, as Nihilist mentioned some of this possibly belongs in another thread. Apologies if I miss anything<br /> <br /> 1. I'm not sure the 1st editions actually can be reconciled. And I'm perfectly OK with that actually. The new editions will more mesh more, from what we've heard, for people who find that an issue<br /> <br /> 2. How far would I compromise on this, never say never but I'm 99% sure I wouldn't as a GM. Novas are simply uninteresting to me and I see no need for A! era powers in 2120+. Neuts and Psions, that's all folks, and there's more than enough there for inventive players to enjoy. Eden didn't happen, the Nihonjin stuff I ignore (although I can see how that would work on AZ, but still - ignored), Process 418 I'll get around to rewriting - or simply have it be a non-event and continue to ignore it, no Novas are left alive in 2120 or are off in their own universe somewhere doing.....um....something. Something I've never really spent any time thinking about O_o. <br /> <br /> 3. The Colony, Divis Mal and other likely insanely powerful types - I simply don't involve them. Back-story, and that's it. Only space-mad abbies remain<br /> <br /> 4. Battleground as the source for 2120 aberrants NPCs? I guess that would be a logical extension of my approach, yeah. I've never really used BG for that, as I hardly ever use Aberrants. If I needed them, then something of the minimal template details in the core book would do or perhaps using BG for inspiration would work for me. Certainly the Aberrant RPG line has zero use in my Aeon games.<br /> <br /> 5. &quot;it must get lonely sometimes with all the adaption and weirding some of us have taken to be head-canon over the year&quot;. Oh heck, no. Everyone's game is their own. I'm perfectly happy with how my games go, and while my eyes glaze over the moment I see the word 'nova', I'm really encouraged if the other game lines do well - it possibly means there's more chance that Aeon gets some love. <br /> <br /> This all does go to show though how many different ways various groups have approached the Trinity games, which gives me hope that it'll get enough steam for plenty of support going forward. I look forward to playing Aberrant (as in modern day Aberrant) and GMing Aeon again. I'll likely be collecting all the books just to support the writers.<br /> <br /> By the way, I think at the end there you're asking about the new rules - codename 'Sardonyx'? If so, OPP have hinted that open development will being soon, and we'll have an opportunity to use a draft of the mechanics to playtest / provide feedback.<br /> <br /> Again, seriously impressed with your detail here Nihilist. **were not worthy** <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T20:48:42-08:00","id":424838,"title":"","content":"If the Venezuela event is still canon in 2e, then a case could be made that the post-event \u00c6on setting <i>should</i> have Nova powers in it. But then, that\u2019s not <i>really</i> the \u00c6on setting anymore; it\u2019s more of a mashup setting (the way <b>Adventure!</b> is, but at a considerably higher power level).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T15:47:13-08:00","id":425739,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=424798#post424798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can we all just take a moment to pause and admire the sheer dedication Nihilist has to this thread. Damn, I need more coffee... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>It's one way to use a weekend, i guess.<br /> -blush-<br /> I would like to clarify that any dedication i betray is entirely to the 2120 'Nova' setting. For all its imbalances and development chasms, the Aberrant system was fun to play for my team - so i have made an effort to not only explain the systems i used, but also the reasons why i so deeply perverted the natural order to need them.<br /> But, i'm way off topic with some of it too - so i apologize for that at least.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=424798#post424798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. I'm not sure the 1st editions actually can be reconciled. And I'm perfectly OK with that actually. The new editions will more mesh more, from what we've heard, for people who find that an issue</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm kind of scared that 'mesh more' will turn into 'level out' - some of these imbalances made for way more interesting game mechanics than the usual vision of calculated risk. A little more extreme-end testing would have been good, but i think the balance found there was compelling with the adjustments i mentioned above too. My only real issue was with cross-over parties - but even that got sorted out with Low-Quantum variants and Adventure!.<br /> Either the Lovecraftian/Justice League vision of Novas are embraced and better regulated, or they are broken relics to be ironed out going forward.<br /> -shrug-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=424798#post424798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. How far would I compromise on this, never say never but I'm 99% sure I wouldn't as a GM</div> </div> </div> </div>Are you happy to accept whatever vision the new authors go with and run with that, or would you stay old school, adapting what you wanted to your 2120?<br /> It sounds kind of like you run an alternate time-line branch from my perspective. I dream of a complex flow chart, where i can actually point out where our two settings diverged in the 200 year journey from the Hammersmith event.<br /> -distances self from idea of alternate time-line flow charts-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=424798#post424798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4. Battleground as the source for 2120 aberrants NPCs? I guess that would be a logical extension of my approach, yeah.</div> </div> </div> </div>Still leaves me wondering how the Colony forces are dealing with heavy vehicle armor adds, but i don't use those rules, so that concern goes up in a puff-of-smoke for me. -smile-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=424798#post424798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way, I think at the end there you're asking about the new rules - codename 'Sardonyx'?</div> </div> </div> </div>Bugger, i'm not paying any attention to this site outside of these forums, but i thought maybe the rules-set was already being tested in other forums. Thanks for the clarification.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CHILL</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=424798#post424798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">**were not worthy** <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks, but stop that, we're all equally worthy fans i gather, there aren't too many of us left in this long drought. We've all had years-long insights into our own familiar slices of the system.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=424838#post424838\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Venezuela event is still canon in 2e, then a case could be made that the post-event \u00c6on setting <i>should</i> have Nova powers in it. But then, that\u2019s not <i>really</i> the \u00c6on setting anymore; it\u2019s more of a mashup setting (the way <b>Adventure!</b> is, but at a considerably higher power level).</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd like to think that Venezuela/Hammersmith are the same family, but different events from what got real 2008 Novas all juiced up. But there could be something else at play that i'm not taking into account. Maybe somewhere down the road (2200 earliest), the Venezuela effect can be refined into something strong enough to not only create Novas (ala 2008), but also create powerful Psionic counterparts and maybe even begin to approach Max Mercers level of exposure to the mysterious third inspiration.<br /> The 2120 setting can survive the Adventure! genres, even purists like CHILL will be able to find some space out there, it's a big universe. But once the Aptitude Psions are faced with irrefutable evidence of greater forces at work - the Psi Orders at least are going to need some serious shaking up.<br /> <br /> But, i've already taken this thread off on a bit of a personal tangent, so now i'll yield the floor back for the discussion of tactical psionic suppression to continue.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T16:42:19-08:00","id":425763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=425739#post425739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm kind of scared that 'mesh more' will turn into 'level out' - some of these imbalances made for way more interesting game mechanics than the usual vision of calculated risk. A little more extreme-end testing would have been good, but i think the balance found there was compelling with the adjustments i mentioned above too. My only real issue was with cross-over parties - but even that got sorted out with Low-Quantum variants and Adventure!.<br /> Either the Lovecraftian/Justice League vision of Novas are embraced and better regulated, or they are broken relics to be ironed out going forward.<br /> -shrug-</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't suspect that this will be too much of an issue at a game mechanics level with the reboot. Its been noted by various writers that 'scaling' is something they're paying very close attention to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=425739#post425739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are you happy to accept whatever vision the new authors go with and run with that, or would you stay old school, adapting what you wanted to your 2120?<br /> It sounds kind of like you run an alternate time-line branch from my perspective. I dream of a complex flow chart, where i can actually point out where our two settings diverged in the 200 year journey from the Hammersmith event.<br /> -distances self from idea of alternate time-line flow charts-</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the game I run is straight from the original Aeon core book, long before Aberrant or Adventure! was published. So, I feel I'm running the 'original' version. The other game lines are (to me) standalone - Three distinct games. In the new Continuum version I want to be able to completely discard (read: relegate to background / history that I can ignore) anything to do with Novas or A! era powers. Simples.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=425739#post425739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Still leaves me wondering how the Colony forces are dealing with heavy vehicle armor adds, but i don't use those rules, so that concern goes up in a puff-of-smoke for me. -smile-</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, no idea. And not something I really think about to be honest. I see the Colony as a boogeyman - something mentioned but never really brought into play or actually needing stats.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=425739#post425739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bugger, i'm not paying any attention to this site outside of these forums, but i thought maybe the rules-set was already being tested in other forums. Thanks for the clarification.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, Scion and the Trinity Continuum will share the same base 'Sardonyx System', but from what we've heard each game line will get its own flavor of that to suit its individual setting / needs. The core system is due ('soon') for beta testing, and the word is it'll be open development so we all get to hack it to pieces. The Monday Meeting notes on this site are the best source for updates on that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=425739#post425739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks, but stop that, we're all equally worthy fans i gather, there aren't too many of us left in this long drought. We've all had years-long insights into our own familiar slices of the system.</div> </div> </div> </div>lol, OK. Still, that was a helluva post :-P<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=425739#post425739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd like to think that Venezuela/Hammersmith are the same family, but different events from what got real 2008 Novas all juiced up. But there could be something else at play that i'm not taking into account. Maybe somewhere down the road (2200 earliest), the Venezuela effect can be refined into something strong enough to not only create Novas (ala 2008), but also create powerful Psionic counterparts and maybe even begin to approach Max Mercers level of exposure to the mysterious third inspiration.<br /> The 2120 setting can survive the Adventure! genres, even purists like CHILL will be able to find some space out there, it's a big universe. But once the Aptitude Psions are faced with irrefutable evidence of greater forces at work - the Psi Orders at least are going to need some serious shaking up.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh sure, I'll be completely ignoring anything non-psion or neutral related in the Aeon setting. Will be interesting to find out how much work I have to do for that to happen <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nihilist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/420749-novas-vs-psions-balance-and-setting?p=425739#post425739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But, i've already taken this thread off on a bit of a personal tangent, so now i'll yield the floor back for the discussion of tactical psionic suppression to continue.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Me too, wrapping this tangent up - I think we'll get a TC core plus modular setting books that can be used to GM the eras entirely separate from each other (my preference), or blended as we prefer (Nihilist's preference). The various noises about the Continuum seem to suggest that this'll be the case. As I've said before, its quite a task to pull this off, but I think there's a good team working on it so I'm hopeful <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}